I had a little more difficulty in this section, specifically with the image exercise Glory of the Garden. For Glory of the Garden,
I ran the command xxd garden.jpg > output.txt and visually inspected the file, due to a lack of consistent strings to find the
flag. After I found it, I realized I was somewhat limited in my searches, as I could have tried grep "CTF" in the output file.
For Enhance!, strings returned data on an SVG file that contained a flag.

Later, in the sleuthkit exercises, Disk, disk, sleuth! gave me pause, but ultimately the "strings" command came through again.

For the extensions exercise, I opened the flag.txt file in several editors, and there was a clue when it came through so cleanly
in a hex editor. exiftool revealed it was actually a PNG, so converting the file type revealed the flag in plain text in the image.

The St3g0 exercise took me a minute until I found out about zsteg. After using zsteg -help, I saw that zsteg -a would try all known
steganography methods. The same process worked for the next exercise as well.

The wireshark exercises were sometimes easy; The Packet primer exercise had the flag visible in a TCP port. The last exercise was
interesting, and combined all of the previous exercises into quite the puzzle! After finally finding the image file, I needed to
install steghide and restart my machine before I could run the command. Oh, the sacrifices we make...
